  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What Is the Golden Ratio Face?</h2>
    <p>The golden ratio, about 1.618, appears in art and architecture as a proportion that feels balanced. Applied to faces, it suggests that certain measurements should relate by 1.618, for example face length to face width. Artists also use the <strong>rule of thirds</strong> (forehead, nose and lower face of equal height) and the <strong>rule of fifths</strong> (the face is five eye-widths across).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ratios" aria-labelledby="ratios-h">
    <h2 id="ratios-h">Ratios the Calculator Measures</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>Facial proportions and their classic targets</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measurement</th><th scope="col">Classic target</th><th scope="col">What it compares</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Face length &divide; face width</th><td>1.618</td><td>Hairline to chin vs cheekbone to cheekbone</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mouth width &divide; nose width</th><td>1.618</td><td>Mouth corners vs nose wings</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lips-to-chin &divide; nose-to-lips</th><td>1.618</td><td>Lower face divisions</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Face width &divide; jaw width</th><td>about 1.25</td><td>Cheekbones vs jaw angles</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Eye width &divide; gap between eyes</th><td>1.0</td><td>Rule of fifths</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Forehead &divide; middle third</th><td>1.0</td><td>Rule of thirds</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lower third &divide; middle third</th><td>1.0</td><td>Rule of thirds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="tips" aria-labelledby="tips-h">
    <h2 id="tips-h">How to Take a Good Photo</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Use a straight-on photo.</strong> Face the camera directly, with the camera at eye level, about an arm’s length or more away. A phone held close to the face distorts the nose and chin.</li><li><strong>Keep a neutral expression.</strong> Relax your face, close your mouth and look straight ahead. Smiles and raised eyebrows are rarely symmetrical.</li><li><strong>Use even light.</strong> Light from the front, such as a window behind the camera. Side light makes one half look different.</li><li><strong>Pull hair back.</strong> Clear the forehead, cheekbones and jawline so you can place the points accurately.</li><li><strong>Place points carefully.</strong> Zoom out the page if needed, drag each dot onto its feature, and use the arrow keys for fine adjustment.</li></ol>
  </section>

  <section id="science" aria-labelledby="science-h">
    <h2 id="science-h">Does the Golden Ratio Predict Beauty?</h2>
    <p>Not very well. When researchers have tested it, faces rated attractive did not cluster around 1.618. A study at the University of Toronto found the most attractive arrangement of features had an eye-to-mouth distance of about 36% of face length and an eye-to-eye distance of about 46% of face width, ratios close to the average face rather than the golden ratio. Averageness, symmetry, skin and expression all play a part, and preferences vary across cultures.</p>
    <p>Treat the score as a fun look at your proportions, not a verdict on your looks.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="symmetry" aria-labelledby="symmetry-h">
    <h2 id="symmetry-h">Golden Ratio vs. Facial Symmetry</h2>
    <p>Proportion and symmetry are different things: a face can match the classic ratios and still be lopsided, or be very symmetrical with a long or wide shape. Check the other side with the <a href="/face-symmetry-test/">face symmetry test</a>. For body proportions, the golden ratio shows up again in the <a href="/shoulder-to-waist-ratio-calculator/">shoulder-to-waist ratio</a>.</p>
  </section>

